What Happens When We Observe More Movies?
The influence of the data depends partly on the sample size.
Recall that the weight given to the data in the posterior mean is:
\[
\frac{n}{\alpha + \beta + n}
\]
As \(n\) increases,
\[
\frac{n}{\alpha + \beta + n}
\]
approaches 1. At the same time, the prior weight
\[
\frac{\alpha + \beta}{\alpha + \beta + n}
\]
approaches 0.
Suppose two samples both have pass rates close to 45%.
A sample with 9 passes out of 20 provides some evidence about \(p\), but its likelihood is fairly wide.
A sample with 90 passes out of 200 has the same observed proportion, but it provides much stronger evidence. Its likelihood is narrower because many more observations support the estimate.
As the sample grows, posteriors produced by different priors tend to move closer together. The data increasingly dominate the starting beliefs. The balance between the prior and the data changes as the amount of evidence changes.
Choose a sample size. Each sample keeps a pass rate near 45%. The three posterior curves narrow and move toward one another as the sample grows.


viewof sampleSize = Inputs.radio(
  ["20 movies", "50 movies", "100 movies", "500 movies"],
  {label: "Sample size", value: "20 movies"}
)







// The same three priors, updated by larger samples with a pass rate near 0.45
{
  const n = Number(sampleSize.split(" ")[0]);
  const passes = Math.round(0.45 * n);
  const failures = n - passes;
  const rate = passes / n;
  const priors = [
    {name: "Lower-pass", alpha: 3, beta: 7, stroke: "#2c3140"},
    {name: "Neutral", alpha: 1, beta: 1, stroke: "#5c5660", dash: "4,3"},
    {name: "Higher-pass", alpha: 7, beta: 3, stroke: "#b32b2f"}
  ];

  function logGamma(z) {
    const c = [
      0.99999999999980993,
      676.5203681218851,
      -1259.1392167224028,
      771.32342877765313,
      -176.61502916214059,
      12.507343278686905,
      -0.13857109526572012,
      9.9843695780195716e-6,
      1.5056327351493116e-7
    ];
    if (z &lt; 0.5) return Math.log(Math.PI / Math.sin(Math.PI * z)) - logGamma(1 - z);
    z -= 1;
    let x = c[0];
    for (let i = 1; i &lt; c.length; i++) x += c[i] / (z + i);
    const t = z + 7.5;
    return 0.5 * Math.log(2 * Math.PI) + (z + 0.5) * Math.log(t) - t + Math.log(x);
  }

  function betaDensity(x, a, b) {
    if (!(x &gt; 0 && x &lt; 1)) return 0;
    const logB = logGamma(a) + logGamma(b) - logGamma(a + b);
    const density = Math.exp((a - 1) * Math.log(x) + (b - 1) * Math.log(1 - x) - logB);
    return Number.isFinite(density) ? density : 0;
  }

  function betaCurve(a, b) {
    return Array.from({length: 401}, (_, i) =&gt; {
      const p = i / 400;
      const x = Math.min(1 - 1e-6, Math.max(1e-6, p));
      return {p, density: betaDensity(x, a, b)};
    });
  }

  function formatMean(x) {
    const three = x.toFixed(3);
    return three.endsWith("0") ? x.toFixed(2) : three;
  }

  const curves = priors.map(prior =&gt; {
    const postAlpha = prior.alpha + passes;
    const postBeta = prior.beta + failures;
    return {
      ...prior,
      mean: postAlpha / (postAlpha + postBeta),
      curve: betaCurve(postAlpha, postBeta)
    };
  });

  const plot = Plot.plot({
    width: Math.max(280, width - 4),
    height: 360,
    marginTop: 16,
    marginRight: 16,
    marginBottom: 48,
    marginLeft: 52,
    x: {
      label: "Population proportion, p",
      domain: [0, 1],
      ticks: [0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, 1]
    },
    y: {label: null, grid: true, nice: true},
    marks: [
      ...curves.map(d =&gt; Plot.line(d.curve, {
        x: "p",
        y: "density",
        stroke: d.stroke,
        strokeWidth: 2.25,
        strokeDasharray: d.dash
      })),
      Plot.ruleY([0])
    ],
    style: {
      background: "transparent",
      color: "#15151E",
      fontFamily: "Helvetica, sans-serif",
      fontSize: "13px"
    }
  });

  const means = curves.map(d =&gt; `${d.name} ${formatMean(d.mean)}`).join(", ");

  return html`&lt;div class="binomial-figure"&gt;
    &lt;p class="binomial-ylabel"&gt;Density&lt;/p&gt;
    ${plot}
    &lt;p class="binomial-readout"&gt;This sample has &lt;strong&gt;${passes}&lt;/strong&gt; passes out of &lt;strong&gt;${n}&lt;/strong&gt;, a rate of &lt;strong&gt;${rate.toFixed(2)}&lt;/strong&gt;. The posterior means are &lt;strong&gt;${means}&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p class="binomial-legend"&gt;&lt;span class="swatch swatch-other" style="margin-left:0"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; Lower-pass &lt;span class="swatch swatch-max"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; Neutral &lt;span class="swatch swatch-observed"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; Higher-pass&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;`;
}

Three Priors Meet the Same Data
The same update now meets the three moviegoers. All three observe the same evidence: 9 passes and 11 failures.

The lower-pass viewpoint
The first moviegoer began with:
\[
p \sim \text{Beta}(3, 7)
\]
Adding the 9 passes and 11 failures gives:
\[
p \mid y \sim \text{Beta}(3 + 9,\ 7 + 11) = \text{Beta}(12, 18)
\]
The prior mean was \(3/10 = 0.30\). The posterior mean is:
\[
E(p \mid y) = 12/(12 + 18) = 0.40
\]


The neutral viewpoint
The second moviegoer is the uniform case above:
\[
p \sim \text{Beta}(1, 1)
\]
\[
p \mid y \sim \text{Beta}(10, 12)
\]
The posterior mean is:
\[
E(p \mid y) = 10/(10 + 12) = 10/22 \approx 0.455
\]


The higher-pass viewpoint
The third moviegoer began with:
\[
p \sim \text{Beta}(7, 3)
\]
\[
p \mid y \sim \text{Beta}(7 + 9,\ 3 + 11) = \text{Beta}(16, 14)
\]
The prior mean was \(7/10 = 0.70\). The posterior mean is:
\[
E(p \mid y) = 16/(16 + 14) = 16/30 \approx 0.533
\]
The data pulls all three people toward the observed pass rate of 0.45. The sample is not large enough to erase their initial disagreement.
The lower-pass moviegoer moves upward from a prior mean of 0.30. The posterior mean, 0.40, remains below the sample proportion.
The neutral moviegoer lets the sample dominate. Beta(1, 1) has concentration \(\alpha + \beta = 2\), so the posterior mean, about 0.455, stays close to 0.45.
The higher-pass moviegoer moves downward from a prior mean of 0.70. The posterior mean, about 0.533, is still above 0.45.
Choose a viewpoint. The graph shows that prior, the likelihood from these 20 movies, and the posterior that combines them. The likelihood is drawn with area 1, so it can sit on the same scale as the two Beta densities. On that scale it is a Beta(10, 12) curve.


