From a Point Estimate to a Posterior Distribution
The posterior mean provides one summary of the posterior, but Bayesian inference does not reduce the answer to a single number.
Under the lower-pass prior:
\[
p \mid y \sim \text{Beta}(12, 18)
\]
The full Beta(12, 18) distribution describes the remaining uncertainty about \(p\) after observing the movies.
An approximate 95% credible interval is:
\[
(0.235,\ 0.577)
\]
The Bayesian interpretation is: given the Beta(3, 7) prior, the Binomial model, its assumptions, and the observed data, there is approximately a 95% posterior probability that \(p\) lies between 0.235 and 0.577.
This is different from the confidence-interval interpretation. A credible interval makes a probability statement about the unknown parameter, conditional on the model and prior.
The posterior can also answer questions about meaningful thresholds. For example:
\[
P(p &lt; 0.50 \mid y) \approx 0.868
\]
Under this prior and model, there is approximately an 86.8% posterior probability that fewer than half of comparable movies pass.
This statement cannot be separated from its assumptions. It would be incomplete to report “there is an 86.8% probability” without identifying the prior, likelihood, population, and data on which that probability depends.
Move the threshold, and choose whether to shade below it or above it. The shaded area is a posterior probability under Beta(12, 18), the lower-pass posterior.


viewof threshold = Inputs.range([0, 1], {
  label: "Threshold",
  value: 0.50,
  step: 0.01
})







viewof side = Inputs.radio(["Below", "Above"], {
  label: "Shade",
  value: "Below"
})







// Posterior probability on one side of a chosen threshold
{
  const postAlpha = 12;
  const postBeta = 18;

  function logGamma(z) {
    const c = [
      0.99999999999980993,
      676.5203681218851,
      -1259.1392167224028,
      771.32342877765313,
      -176.61502916214059,
      12.507343278686905,
      -0.13857109526572012,
      9.9843695780195716e-6,
      1.5056327351493116e-7
    ];
    if (z &lt; 0.5) return Math.log(Math.PI / Math.sin(Math.PI * z)) - logGamma(1 - z);
    z -= 1;
    let x = c[0];
    for (let i = 1; i &lt; c.length; i++) x += c[i] / (z + i);
    const t = z + 7.5;
    return 0.5 * Math.log(2 * Math.PI) + (z + 0.5) * Math.log(t) - t + Math.log(x);
  }

  function betaDensity(x, a, b) {
    if (!(x &gt; 0 && x &lt; 1)) return 0;
    const logB = logGamma(a) + logGamma(b) - logGamma(a + b);
    const density = Math.exp((a - 1) * Math.log(x) + (b - 1) * Math.log(1 - x) - logB);
    return Number.isFinite(density) ? density : 0;
  }

  // For integer a and b, the Beta(a, b) cdf equals a binomial sum.
  function betaCDF(x, a, b) {
    if (x &lt;= 0) return 0;
    if (x &gt;= 1) return 1;
    const total = a + b - 1;
    let sum = 0;
    for (let k = a; k &lt;= total; k++) {
      let logCoefficient = 0;
      for (let i = 0; i &lt; k; i++) logCoefficient += Math.log(total - i) - Math.log(i + 1);
      sum += Math.exp(logCoefficient + k * Math.log(x) + (total - k) * Math.log(1 - x));
    }
    return Math.min(1, Math.max(0, sum));
  }

  const curve = Array.from({length: 201}, (_, i) =&gt; {
    const p = i / 200;
    const x = Math.min(1 - 1e-6, Math.max(1e-6, p));
    return {p, density: betaDensity(x, postAlpha, postBeta)};
  });
  const edge = {
    p: threshold,
    density: betaDensity(Math.min(1 - 1e-6, Math.max(1e-6, threshold)), postAlpha, postBeta)
  };
  const shaded = side === "Below"
    ? [...curve.filter(d =&gt; d.p &lt; threshold), edge]
    : [edge, ...curve.filter(d =&gt; d.p &gt; threshold)];
  const probability = side === "Below"
    ? betaCDF(threshold, postAlpha, postBeta)
    : 1 - betaCDF(threshold, postAlpha, postBeta);
  const percent = probability * 100;
  const percentText = percent &gt;= 1
    ? `${percent.toFixed(1)}%`
    : percent &gt;= 0.01
      ? `${percent.toFixed(2)}%`
      : percent &gt; 0
        ? "&lt; 0.01%"
        : "0%";
  const relation = side === "Below" ? "below" : "above";

  const plot = Plot.plot({
    width: Math.max(280, width - 4),
    height: 360,
    marginTop: 16,
    marginRight: 16,
    marginBottom: 48,
    marginLeft: 52,
    x: {
      label: "Population proportion, p",
      domain: [0, 1],
      ticks: [0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, 1]
    },
    y: {label: null, grid: true, nice: true},
    marks: [
      Plot.areaY(shaded, {
        x: "p",
        y: "density",
        fill: "#b32b2f",
        fillOpacity: 0.18,
        curve: "linear"
      }),
      Plot.line(curve, {
        x: "p",
        y: "density",
        stroke: "#2c3140",
        strokeWidth: 2.25
      }),
      Plot.ruleX([threshold], {
        stroke: "#b32b2f",
        strokeWidth: 2
      }),
      Plot.ruleY([0])
    ],
    style: {
      background: "transparent",
      color: "#15151E",
      fontFamily: "Helvetica, sans-serif",
      fontSize: "13px"
    }
  });

  return html`&lt;div class="binomial-figure"&gt;
    &lt;p class="binomial-ylabel"&gt;Density&lt;/p&gt;
    ${plot}
    &lt;p class="binomial-readout"&gt;Under the Beta(12, 18) posterior, the probability that &lt;em&gt;p&lt;/em&gt; is ${relation} ${threshold.toFixed(2)} is &lt;strong&gt;${percentText}&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p class="binomial-legend"&gt;&lt;span class="swatch swatch-other" style="margin-left:0"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; Posterior &lt;span class="swatch swatch-observed"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; Probability ${relation} the threshold&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;`;
}

Bayesian learning can occur sequentially. Suppose we begin with the lower-pass prior:
\[
p \sim \text{Beta}(3, 7)
\]
The 20 movies still contain 9 passes and 11 failures. The order below is one possible order for those same movies. The final posterior does not depend on that order.
The first movie passes. The updated distribution becomes:
\[
p \mid \text{first movie} \sim \text{Beta}(4, 7)
\]
The second movie fails:
\[
p \mid \text{first two movies} \sim \text{Beta}(4, 8)
\]
Each passing movie adds one to the first parameter. Each failing movie adds one to the second parameter.
After all 20 movies, the result is:
\[
p \mid \text{all 20 movies} \sim \text{Beta}(12, 18)
\]
The posterior after one stage becomes the prior for the next stage. Updating one movie at a time produces the same final posterior as updating with all 20 movies at once, assuming the same model and starting prior.
Move through the movies one at a time. The density is the updated distribution after the movies revealed so far. The lower graph tracks its mean, with a band one standard deviation on either side.


